"""Live-captured terminal fixtures for the review-loop tests (t1159_2).

Provenance: captured 2026-08-12 from a real Claude Code 2.1.228 session
driven in a private tmux server, through the monitor's own capture path
(``capture-pane -p -e -S -30`` + ``TmuxMonitor._finalize_capture``) — the
same path production reads. The ``*_RAW`` fixtures keep their ANSI escapes
on purpose: the readiness detector must distinguish the composer's dim
placeholder hint from typed text, which ANSI-stripping erases. The stripped
fixtures are full 30-line captures at production depth (minimonitor's
``capture_lines`` default), so the classifier tests compare exactly what the
app compares.

States: at-rest (empty composer, dim hint), typed (unsubmitted composer
text), streaming (bare composer + animated spinner), AskUserQuestion dialog
in two selection states, ExitPlanMode plan-approval dialog in two selection
states plus a post-revision render. Regenerate by re-running the t1159_2
pre-phase probe against a live pane when Claude Code's UI changes
(patterns are maintained in-place — the prompt_patterns.py practice, t1474).

**Codex fixtures (``CODEX_*``, t1509).** Captured 2026-08-14 from a live
codex-cli 0.146.0 / gpt-5.6-terra session on a private tmux socket (120x30)
through the same ``capture-pane -p -e`` path, then trimmed to the last 15
non-blank-terminated lines — the exact window ``monitor_core.capture_raw_tail``
reads in production, so a fixture cannot pass on content the app would never
see. States: at-rest (dim placeholder hint), typed, working, permission
dialog, question widget, and the startup update prompt.

``CODEX_UPDATE_PROMPT_RAW`` is load-bearing rather than decorative: **no**
codex prompt pattern matches it, so it is the fixture that proves readiness is
excluded *structurally* (an option row rendered with the composer glyph) and
not merely by pattern coverage. Do not drop it when refreshing the others.

Two measured properties the Codex fixtures encode, both of which the detector
depends on: the placeholder hint ROTATES between sessions (so the hint text is
never pinned — only its dim styling), and the working/running status line stays
on screen WHILE the pane is parked at a permission dialog (so `dialog` must
outrank `working`).

**OpenCode fixtures (``OPENCODE_*``, t1520).** Captured 2026-08-16 from a live
opencode 1.18.18 / GPT-5.4 session on a private tmux socket (120x30) through the
same ``capture-pane -p -e`` path.

Stored at their **full captured extent, deliberately NOT trimmed to 15 lines**
like the Codex set. ``capture-pane -S -15`` carries no ``-E``, so it reads from
15 lines back to the BOTTOM OF THE VISIBLE PANE; OpenCode is an
alternate-screen TUI (measured ``alternate_on=1``, ``history_size=0``), so
there is no scrollback and the capture is the whole visible pane for any
``-S`` value. The 15 is a scrollback floor, not a window size. Trimming to 15
lines was measured to DROP the fresh-session hint row entirely, which would
leave the gray-hint negative control passing vacuously.

Two at-rest fixtures on purpose: the gray placeholder hint is present on a
FRESH session and GONE after the first turn, so
``OPENCODE_AT_REST_AFTER_TURN_RAW`` is the durable case and
``OPENCODE_AT_REST_FRESH_RAW`` exists to exercise the hint-subtraction path.
There is deliberately no bare ``OPENCODE_AT_REST_RAW``: the two differ in the
way that matters, so nobody should be able to reach for "the" at-rest fixture
without choosing one.

Three of these are load-bearing rather than decorative:

* ``OPENCODE_PERMISSION_RAW`` -- the dialog is ALSO a ``┃``-gutter box and
  CONTAINS BLANK GUTTER ROWS, so it is the fixture on which a naive "a blank
  gutter row exists" rule false-positives. It carries no ``╹▀▀▀`` border and no
  status row, which is what excludes it structurally.
* ``OPENCODE_PALETTE_RAW`` -- the ``ctrl+p`` command palette is an OVERLAY that
  leaves the composer box rendered intact, so the structural half cannot see
  it; it is excluded by the ``opencode_palette`` pattern alone. It is the
  counter-example to reading the permission dialog's structural exclusion as a
  general property.
* ``OPENCODE_WORKING_NO_FOOTER_ROOM_RAW`` -- a REAL capture (pane height 6, not
  a truncation) of a genuinely working agent, verified against the opencode
  process tree's CPU time. With no room below the border the working footer is
  not rendered at all AND the capture is byte-identical across samples, so both
  brakes fail; it is the fixture pinning
  ``_OPENCODE_MIN_LINES_BELOW_BORDER``. Do not "fix" it by recapturing at a
  normal size.

Measured properties the OpenCode fixtures encode: there is NO SGR-dim anywhere
in the composer (the hint is truecolor gray ``38;2;128;128;128``, typed text is
``238;238;238``), the working footer's glyph run ROTATES between ``⬝`` and
``■``, and -- unlike Codex -- OpenCode does NOT keep a working indicator on
screen while parked at a dialog, so no dialog-outranks-working fixture exists
for this agent.

**Native-dialog boundary fixtures (``*_SEL1``/``*_SEL2``/``*_LATER``, t1518).**
Captured 2026-08-17 on a private socket at 120x30 through the same
``capture-pane -p -e`` path, against codex-cli 0.146.0 (gpt-5.6-terra) and
OpenCode 1.18.18 (GPT-5.4). Stored at FULL captured extent -- these are
followed-pane frames, which production reads at ``capture_lines`` (200), and the
``CODEX_*`` trim-to-15 habit above belongs to the shadow-side tail, not here.

They come in threes because a boundary row needs BOTH directions plus a
neighbour: ``SEL1``/``SEL2`` are the same live dialog with the option cursor
moved and nothing else (must classify as no-fire), and ``LATER`` is the same
dialog kind captured after several further turns, so the transcript ABOVE the
boundary has changed (must classify ``WORK``).

The two agents encode DIFFERENT no-fire mechanisms, which is why both pairs
exist:

* ``CODEX_EXEC_APPROVAL_SEL1/SEL2_RAW`` differ in the STRIPPED text -- Codex
  draws its cursor as a ``›`` glyph on the option rows -- so they exercise the
  boundary comparison itself and classify ``SELECTION_ONLY``.
* ``OPENCODE_PERMISSION_SEL1/SEL2_RAW`` are byte-identical once stripped:
  OpenCode renders selection purely as ANSI styling, so they classify
  ``NO_CHANGE`` *before* any boundary is consulted. **They are not
  interchangeable with the Codex pair and the difference is not an accident of
  capture** -- verified against the RAW frames, which do differ. An earlier
  measurement pass sent ``Tab`` (the widget hints ``⇆ select``) and got a
  byte-identical RAW frame back, i.e. the key never registered; that dead
  keypress would have looked exactly like this property. ``⇆`` is left/right
  here, ``tab`` being globally bound to "agents".

**Claude tool-permission dialog fixtures (``CLAUDE_PERMISSION_*``, t1540).**
Captured 2026-08-17 on a private socket against Claude Code 2.1.233 (Haiku 4.5)
through the same ``capture-pane -p -e ... -S -200`` path, 5 reps per geometry.
Stored at FULL captured extent, same followed-pane rule as the t1518 set. Claude
draws its option cursor as a ``❯`` glyph, so the SEL pairs differ in the STRIPPED
text and classify ``SELECTION_ONLY`` -- the Codex mechanism, not the OpenCode one.

THREE geometries, because pane height changes which KIND is reported and this is
the only fixture family where that happens:

* ``CLAUDE_PERMISSION_SEL1/SEL2/LATER_RAW`` -- 120x30. Full option list; the
  boundary question renders at -7, outside ``_PROMPT_DETECTION_TAIL_LINES`` (6),
  so the bottom-anchored ``claude_help_bar`` is the reported kind.
* ``CLAUDE_PERMISSION_COMPACT_*`` -- 120x14, the height a same-window shadow
  split leaves. Same regime as above; kept because 120x30 is NOT the geometry
  the review loop runs in, and a row valid only there would be untested where it
  matters.
* ``CLAUDE_PERMISSION_SHORT_SEL1/SEL2_RAW`` -- 120x6. Claude truncates the option
  list to the selected row, lifting the question to -5, INSIDE the tail window;
  ``claude_proceed`` is listed first and matching is first-wins, so it becomes
  the reported kind. This is why ``claude_proceed`` carries a boundary row at all
  -- unlike ``codex_yes_proceed`` it is reachable in production, measured here.

``SEL2`` is deliberately the **option-2** frame ("Yes, and always allow ..."),
not option 3: on 2.1.233 that is the one option whose help bar drops the
``Tab to amend`` affordance. Before t1540 widened ``claude_help_bar`` that frame
reported NO kind, and the pair classified ``WORK`` -- a pure cursor move reading
as agent work. Swapping in an option-3 frame would make the pair pass without
exercising the defect at all.

``CLAUDE_NO_DIALOG_AT_REST_RAW`` is a real at-rest capture from the same session.
It is the scope control: forced to the ``claude_help_bar`` kind it must still
fail to anchor, proving the boundary is scoped to the permission dialog and that
every other help-bar surface keeps returning ``UNKNOWN``.
"""

